Think of a vending machine. When you walk up to it, you do not hand it anything — you just press a button and it does its job. The machine takes **no input from you** to start up; it simply runs.

In C, `main(void)` works the same way. The word `void` inside the parentheses is your way of telling the compiler: "this function takes **no arguments** — nothing needs to be handed to it for it to start running."

```c
int main(void)  // "Start here, and expect nothing handed in"
{
    printf("hello, world\n");
}
```

The `int` at the front is a separate idea — it describes what `main` *gives back* to the operating system when it finishes, which you will explore soon.

So here is a question to check your understanding: if `void` in the parentheses means "takes nothing in," what do you think it might mean when you see `void` *before* a function's name — like `void meow(void)`? [1](https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/2025/notes/1/)

A `for` loop in C++ has three parts in its header — an initializer, a condition, and an increment — and executes its body repeatedly until the condition is false [1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-s096-introduction-to-c-and-c-january-iap-2013/pages/lectures-and-assignments/):

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // body executes while i < 10
}
```

If you want to explore the theory behind control flow more deeply, toggle "Study Assist" mode — but what specific part of your current assignment are you trying to use a loop for?

Every C program needs a `main` function as its entry point — the compiler looks for it first when your program runs, which is exactly why you got that linker error. [1](https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/2025/notes/1/)

A minimal skeleton looks like this:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{

}
```

If you want to go deeper on program structure, toggle **Study Assist** mode. Now, looking at your empty `hello.c` — what do you think should go *inside* the curly braces to print `hello, world`?

